  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/64/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_22.png",
  "plot_summary": "The story opens with Reed running tests on Sue's invisibility power.&nbsp; Predictably, Johnny and Ben get into a tussle that results in the Thing trying to douse Johnny with a canister of chemical foam.&nbsp; Sue is in the direct line of fire, and suddenly, an invisible barrier blocks the foam!&nbsp; Reed is thrilled that she has \"created a shield of invisible energy!\"&nbsp; For the first time, Sue realizes that her invisibility power is a form of energy that she can fashion into a protective screen!\nAs Reed encourages her, she fashions a more powerful invisible screen that both Ben and Johnny attempt to penetrate, to no avail.&nbsp; Reed thinks this is only the beginning!&nbsp; Later in the issue, Sue discovers she can make other things invisible too (while at the same time not being able to maintain her own invisibility.)\n\nAt that moment, a NYPD police officer enters, who's obviously intended to be none other than Francis Muldoon (Fred Gwynne) from the TV show <i>Car 54 Where Are You?</i> (which had been canceled by the time this issue came out, but that fact was perhaps as yet unknown to Lee and Kirby at the time of creation).&nbsp; He's here to report a complaint about the ICBM they keep on the premises, which he thinks violates zoning laws.&nbsp; Suddenly the nearby phones ring, and when Ben answers it, he's beset with complaints about this and that - noise, sudden shaking of the building, scaring cats, weapons on hand, etc.&nbsp; The FF are making the building undesirable for the other tenants!&nbsp; And a sculptor shows up as well to complain about his sculpture getting melted when the Human Torch flew past his window!&nbsp; The Women's Canasta society shows up to ask them to stop their \"dangerous experiments.\"&nbsp; Reed even fields a call from a \"Mrs. Frobush\" where he says \"I don't know how you got our number, but <i>honest</i>...this <i>isn't</i> Sam's delicatessen!!\" (Heh, which to my knowledge is the first time \"Frobush\" is mentioned in a comic - later presumably morphing to the more familiar \"Forbush\" (namely Irving).&nbsp; But Reed's exasperated face is <i>classic</i> - see panel 3 on page 7!).\n\nAfter Sue helps get rid of the intruders, Reed realizes that Sue never need worry again about feeling as if she didn't contribute enough to the team!&nbsp; He even goes so far to suggest that she might actually be the \"star member!\"&nbsp; But with all the complaints about what they do constantly in the building, Reed also realizes that they do indeed need to find a more secluded spot for some of their larger experiments.&nbsp; He sees a pamphlet about a \"small deserted island for sale off the coast of New Jersey\" (as if that could <i>ever</i> be the case), and they take off to go have a look at it.\n\nAs they approach, they see <i>why</i> it's deserted - surrounded by \"an impenetrable barrier reef.\"&nbsp; It's an eerie place to be sure, and seemingly on cue, their \"u-car\" is pulled underwater, and a figure appears in the shadows.&nbsp; He rushes into a cave as the four pursue.&nbsp; Lo and behold, it's their first enemy, the Mole Man!&nbsp; He welcomes them to his \"kingdom of Subterranea!\"&nbsp; He lured them here with all those complaints from the building tenants, and now they fall into this trap, a \"cage\" where the walls are radioactive!&nbsp; (As they fall, Ben laments that his \"dear ol' Aunt Petunia\" warned him about the company he kept - her first <i>ever</i> mention! - see note below.)\n\nWhen Ben heads over to the wall to climb it, Reed pulls him back, calling him both a \"muscle-bound numbskull\" and a \"lumbering deadhead!\" (<i>Wow, pretty crude words for Reed!</i>)&nbsp; Reed is obviously upset at himself for leading them into this.&nbsp; Johnny even thinks he's never seen Reed so grim.\n\nThe Mole Man then explains how he escaped underground when Monster Isle was destroyed (back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1]).&nbsp; And now he's planning to cause major cities to sink below the Earth, making the US and Soviet Union think the other was responsible, leading to a surface war!&nbsp; But as he reaches for the control device, he discovers that he cannot touch it!&nbsp; Sue has projected an invisible force bubble around it!&nbsp; Reed suggests she surround them instead so that they can approach the wall, shielded by Sue's invisible energy.&nbsp; (<i>I don't know why Johnny didn't just fly directly out of there - go figure</i>!)\n\nNow outside of the cage, they attack!&nbsp; But each is drawn into a room designed to hold them, and the next couple of pages show each one using their own ingenuity to escape.&nbsp; But even as they emerge to face him again, he plunges the room into darkness and uses his \"radar sense\" to evade them (sure, enough, as advertised in the editorial blurb, this was indeed mentioned in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1]).&nbsp; Reed and Ben fumble about in the darkness for a bit before the Torch sheds some light on things.\n\nReed orders them back to the now-discovered \"u-car,\" seeming to be really stressed about it, telling them they need to go <i>now!</i>  The Mole Man makes his way back to his control device, intending to sink the cities as advertised, but he learns (literally on the very last panel) that Reed has \"rearranged the main circuit wiring so that nothing sinks beneath the Earth's surface except the Mole Man's isle <i>itself</i>, destroying all of his complex scientific equipment in one man inundating tide of water...\" as the FF escape.\n\nYes, all that was in the <i>very last panel</i>!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "A <i>very</i> difficult one to grade.&nbsp; On the positive side, we have the Invisible Girl <i>finally</i> being given powers enough to actually make a significant contribution to the group.&nbsp; Up until this point, Lee and Kirby seemed to have to go out of their way to find something she could do.&nbsp; That will no longer be necessary, as she has been given a nice set of abilities.&nbsp; They did a great job introducing them and showing off how she can use them.\n\nAlso on the positive side was particularly playful way that Ben and Johnny were bickering (I especially loved panel 4 on page 2), the cameo appearance by Fred Gwynne (later of course better known for playing Herman Munster), and especially the absurdity of the complaints of the nearby tenants and phone calls asking for \"Sam's Delicatessen\" that made me laugh out loud, particularly Reed's face when he was talking to Mrs. Frobush on the phone.&nbsp; <i>Well</i> done!&nbsp; I know the Mole Man takes credit for it later in the issue, but you <i>do</i> have to wonder why this hadn't actually come up before - I suspect he didn't really have to twist anyone's arms too much.\n\nOn the negative side, the Mole Man?&nbsp; <i>Really</i>?&nbsp; I know it was advertised as being the FF vs their very first foe, but he has never been a particularly memorable one, and his plan to sink the cities and start WW3?&nbsp; Seems like a lot of work!&nbsp; And the fact that no one noticed that the city was being set up on a huge platform to sink in the first place?&nbsp; I'm not buying it.\n\nThe death traps were just that - death traps, which are visually clever, but nevertheless formulaic, particularly when it comes to the FF - you can almost count on it being either a page or a half-page of each one figuring out how to elude them.\n\nAnd as for the ending - \"<i>Holy lack of space, Batman</i>!\"&nbsp; Wow, that was about as abrupt an ending as I've ever seen before, requiring the last panel to have so much text added to it just to explain what happened!&nbsp; It's as if Kirby lost track of things when he was busy showing them getting out of their various death traps and suddenly realized \"Dang, this is the last page!\"&nbsp; I've never seen so much crammed into one page to wrap things up.&nbsp; Poor planning, which affected the final grade.&nbsp; (And I guess New Jersey is out one \"deserted island\" off the coast to boot!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe mixed quality of the issue relegates it to mere mid-grade status.&nbsp; Not a <i>bad</i> issue, and it certainly had its moments, but hardly a great one either!\n\nFinally, note that the Marvel database posts that the first mention of Aunt Petunia is <i>Fantastic Four</i> #25, which is clearly incorrect since she is mentioned in this issue.&nbsp; (I learned this when I was researching to find out if this was indeed her first mention - it is - you can see the text for yourself!)\n\nThis issue is also notable for being the first time Ben hollers out his famous \"It's <i>clobberin</i><i>' time</i>!\" war-cry (on page 15).",
  "letters_page": "This issue features a letter from \"Dave Cockrum\" (at that point apparently a college student in Carbondale, IL), who of course would go on to become a well-known artist and later writer, and a co-creator of the New X-Men!\n\nAnother letter from a guy named \"Roy Thomas,\" who complains that he went to the store and ended up spending a whole $1.95 on comic books -- all of them Marvel!&nbsp; And he points out that he buys two copies of each one!&nbsp; (I hope he held onto them!)\n\nAnd I liked the comment Stephen Erickson made about the <i>Incredible Hulk</i> -- that the first two issues were great \"and ended up a waste of paper.\"&nbsp; (<i>Haha</i>, so true!&nbsp; But I wouldn't have even granted them two issues -- the whole title just stunk!)\n\nThe Announcements section explains why they use a limited number of artists (essentially consisting of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko and Don Heck at this early stage).",
  "ownership": "My own VG/FN or so copy of this book came as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $20.&nbsp; It ultimately sold on eBay for $139 in Dec 2024, netting me over $98.",
